{"target_in_paper":""This raises the question of whether the variational method is a well defined procedure for finding the effective mass. As far as we know it has not been shown that this corresponds to calculating the real part of the complex excitation energy discussed above." (Section III, near end of Excited States discussion)","completeness_score":6,"correct_elements":"The draft correctly identifies that the effective mass M* is obtained by minimising a truncated, finite-momentum variational trial state and fitting the resulting energy shift Eₚ=E₀+p²/(2M*), and it captures the open question posed in the paper about the mathematical well-definedness of this procedure and whether it yields the real part of the complex excitation energy of the dressed impurity.","missing_details":["Specification of the exact variational trial wave function (Eq (25) generalised to finite total momentum) and its truncation to at most N particle-hole excitations (Sec II.1)","Definition of E^{(N)}(p) as the variationally minimised energy in the truncated Hilbert space at total momentum p","Clarification of the order of limits: N→∞ (removal of truncation) and p→0 (small momentum) and whether they commute","Definition of the exact complex excitation energy E_full(p)=E₀+p²/(2M*)−iΓ(p)/2 with Γ(p)=O(p⁴) for small p (Sec III first paragraph)","Condition p<p_c(N) needed for finite N to yield a non-infinite M*, and its dependence on N","Reference to working in the thermodynamic limit and the need for a well-defined fitting region in momentum space"],"refined_question":"Consider the Hamiltonian (Eq (1)) of a single impurity interacting via a wide Feshbach resonance with an ideal zero-temperature 3D Fermi gas. Let Ψ^{(N)}(p) be the variational trial wave function (generalisation of Eq (25) to total momentum p) truncated to at most N particle-hole excitations, and let E^{(N)}(p)=⟨Ψ^{(N)}(p)|H|Ψ^{(N)}(p)⟩ be its minimal energy at momentum p. Does the double limit

   M^*_{var}=\lim_{p→0}\lim_{N→∞} rac{2[E^{(N)}(p)−E₀]}{p²}

exist and yield a unique finite value? Moreover, does this variationally obtained M^*_{var} agree with the real part of the complex quasiparticle excitation energy E_full(p)=E₀+p²/(2M*)−iΓ(p)/2 of the long-lived dressed impurity, as defined by the pole of the exact Green’s function (with Γ(p)=O(p⁴))?","equivalent_formulation":null,"justification":"The paper explicitly poses this open problem in Section III after showing that truncated finite-momentum variational ansätze can spuriously yield infinite M* and noting the lack of proof that the variational fit corresponds to Re E_full(p)([ar5iv.org](https://ar5iv.org/pdf/1404.3220)). My draft captured the essence but omitted the precise definition of the trial state (Eq (25)), the limit ordering (N→∞, p→0), the definition of the exact complex excitation energy, and the condition p<p_c(N) for finite truncations. The refined question incorporates all necessary notation, definitions, and quantifiers to be self-contained and faithful to the paper’s intent.","citations":[{"section":"II.1 Choice of groundstate wave function","label":"discussion of Eq (25) generalised to finite momentum and truncation to N excitations","page":"4"},{"section":"III Excited states","label":"definition of complex excitation energy for small p (real and imaginary parts)","page":"7"},{"section":"III Excited states","label":"open problem whether variational procedure is well defined and computes Re of complex excitation energy","page":"8"}]}
